INT. FAMILY MINIVAN, PARKED OUTSIDE GRANDMA'S HOUSE - DAY

Rain taps the windshield. The seats are littered with crayons and fast-food bags. A tiny air freshener swings from the mirror.

DAD, ROBERT OYELARAN, forty-five, grips the wheel and stares at the house. MOM, WENDY OYELARAN, forty-three, in the passenger seat, adjusts her hair in the visor mirror. In the back, TEEN, MAYA, fifteen, scrolls her phone, earbuds in, and LITTLE BROTHER BENNY, seven, hugs a stuffed rabbit.

WENDY
(to the car)
Okay. Everyone. Smile, be polite, no complaining.

BENNY
Do I have to hug her?

ROBERT
Yes.

BENNY
She smells like cabbage.

WENDY
Benny.

MAYA
(pulling an earbud)
Can we skip it this year?

ROBERT
No, Maya.

MAYA
(muttering)
Great.

WENDY
Robert, you remember what we agreed.

ROBERT
No politics.

WENDY
No politics, no complaints about the casserole.

ROBERT
She makes it on purpose.

WENDY
Robert.

ROBERT
Fine.

The front door of the house opens. GRANDMA waves from the porch in a pink cardigan. Everyone in the van freezes, then waves back at once, teeth bared in unison.

BENNY
She's got the casserole.

ROBERT
(under his breath)
I know.

The doors open. They file out into the rain, Maya last, dragging her feet. She pauses to pocket her earbuds.

MAYA
(whispering)
Love you guys.

WENDY
What?

MAYA
Nothing. Let's go.
